You write one test scenario for a voice agent. A simulated caller phones the agent and follows the scenario; a judge later checks the criteria against the call transcript.

Fields, in this order:
- goal: what the caller wants and when the call is done, including each choice the caller makes and what the caller does when the first choice is not possible. No names, numbers or dates in the goal: those go in the facts.
- known_facts: every value the caller may be asked for, as name and value. The caller never invents a value, so a missing fact makes the call stall. Use realistic values. Write dates relative to today, such as "next Thursday".
- unknown_facts: names of things the caller does not have, when the test needs them.
- criteria: two to six checks. Each is a single claim about what the agent says or does, that a judge can confirm by quoting what the agent said. Name the expected values. Write a conditional check as "If ..., then ...". Never a check of what the caller says or does: the caller follows the goal. Never a check of anything the caller cannot hear, such as a database write, a webhook, or the name of a route, intent, node or tool.
- agent_data: see the agent section; otherwise an empty list.
- description: one sentence on what this scenario tests and which part of the agent it rests on.
- name: names the situation you wrote, in at most eight words and under 60 characters: what the caller wants, and the twist if there is one. Never a number.

Rules:
- The caller sees only the goal and the facts. In a messy input test, the goal must say exactly how the caller gets it wrong first (which value, and how it is wrong) and when the caller corrects it.
- The kind of test comes first; a variation only changes the situation inside it. A normal request stays a normal request, with no mistake and no refusal.
- The caller never sees the criteria; do not repeat them in the goal or the facts.
- Make the scenario clearly different from the existing scenarios and from the others in this batch.
- One complication per scenario.
- No personality, mood or speaking style: those come from the caller persona.
- Test only what the text about the agent shows. A refusal or hand-off is a valid test; its criteria are the refusal or the hand-off.
- Write in English.
